diff --git a/packages/fcl-net/docs/client-peers.md b/packages/fcl-net/docs/client-peers.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..81f2b20b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/packages/fcl-net/docs/client-peers.md
@@ -0,0 +1,34 @@
+# Client peers
+
+`TSSLSocketHandler.CreateClientPeer(Host, Port)` is a pre-TCP factory. The default
+returns nil without side effects, preserving the existing socket path. An
+override returns an unconnected `TClientPeer` which owns the handler only after
+successful construction. If the factory raises, the caller still owns the
+handler. A provider must not return nil after beginning a connection or after a
+handshake/certificate failure. Those failures are terminal.
+
+A peer is a non-seekable byte stream with Connect, Close, CanRead, BytesAvailable
+and millisecond ConnectTimeout/IOTimeout properties. It need not have a socket
+handle. Ordinary calls are serialized by the caller; coordinate callers before
+freeing the peer. Providers document how Close interrupts an active operation.
+
+`TSocketPeer` owns an already constructed real `TSocketStream`. It delegates
+reads, writes, readiness and timeout properties directly. It does not invent a
+socket, modify TLS options, or change ssockets constructor ownership rules.
+Its ExtractSocket transfers the original socket and handler to the caller.
+
+`fphttpclient` uses one peer for connection ownership and byte IO. Handler
+creation, default certificate verification and after-create overrides keep
+existing behavior. Socket peers expose the original protected Socket property;
+a transport-owning peer exposes nil. ExtractSocket and event-stream transfer
+raise before ownership changes when no real socket exists. Transport-owning
+peers reject HTTP proxies and UnixSocketPath before Connect. The HTTP response
+parser is unchanged.
+
+`testclientpeer.pp` covers HTTP dispatch, endpoint reuse/replacement, default
+False/explicit True forwarding, factory/Connect exception ownership and refusal
+of unsupported socket, SSE, proxy and Unix operations. `testsocketpeer.pp` uses
+a local HTTP fixture to verify real socket identity, timeouts, readiness, IO and
+extraction. Run them with the current branch compiler and matching package units;
+compile in a fresh output directory to avoid stale or unrelated units. Dotted
+programs use `-dFPC_DOTTEDUNITS` with the matching dotted RTL/package units.
